/* label.sym -- C-spellable names for assembler-local `.L` data labels.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
 *
 * Hand-written asm in this tree refers to its data tables by the labels the
 * original disassembly produced -- `.La0108`, `.L10` and so on. A `.L` name is
 * not a C identifier, so an elevated function that indexes one of those tables
 * has no way to name it.
 *
 * The overlays already solve this, each in its own overlay.ld:
 *
 *     _TBL_L10 = .L10;
 *
 * and the C side spells it `extern short L10[] __asm__("_TBL_L10");`. This
 * file is the same device for code outside the overlays, which links through
 * stage1.ld and had nowhere to put such an assignment.
 *
 * An absolute assignment in a linker script EMITS NO BYTES, so an entry here
 * cannot change the ROM. A wrong NAME costs nothing; a wrong TARGET fails to
 * link.
 *
 * THE BAR FOR ADDING ONE: the label must already be `.global` in the asm that
 * defines it (otherwise the assembler discards it and the link fails), and the
 * C file must genuinely need to index the table. Do not add an alias to avoid
 * understanding what a table is -- name it properly in the .c once its meaning
 * is known.
 *
 * BEWARE SHORT NAMES. docs/elevation.md records a label-number COLLISION: a
 * cross-object `.LN` with a small N can be captured by gcc's own branch labels
 * in the referencing object, which is silently wrong code and invisible to
 * tryc because it lives in the relocation table. The `_TBL_` prefix exists to
 * make the C-side name uncollidable; keep it.
 */

/* Func_8097384 indexes an eight-entry table of pointers selected by a chain of
 * flag tests. Defined and exported at asm/rom_8a000/rom_97384_c_c_c_c.s:5. */
_TBL_a0108 = .La0108;

/* LoadUIBanner (0x0801a32c) selects a graphics-table base with a four-way
 * switch, and its literal pool holds FOUR entries that are all the address of
 * Data_31864.
 *
 * Four identical pool words cannot come from one symbol referenced four times.
 * gcc-2.96 tail-merges identical basic blocks, so one symbol in all four arms
 * collapses the function to NINE instructions and folds the switch away
 * entirely; the ROM has TWENTY-NINE with all four arms intact. Both numbers are
 * measured. So the source referenced four DISTINCT names that resolve to one
 * address, and these are three of them (the fourth arm is the default and keeps
 * Data_31864 itself).
 *
 * WHAT IS PROVEN AND WHAT IS NOT. That there were four distinct symbols at this
 * address is a fact about the ROM, with an in-function control. What they were
 * CALLED is not recoverable, and these names are placeholders chosen to say only
 * what is known -- the selector values 1..3 that reach them. Renaming them later
 * costs nothing; the assignments emit no bytes.
 *
 * Assigned from the symbol rather than from 0x08031864 so they cannot drift.
 *
 * This stretches this file's charter, which is `.L` labels. It is here because
 * the header's reason for the file holds exactly -- code outside the overlays
 * links through stage1.ld and has nowhere else to put an absolute assignment.
 */
_UIBANNER_1 = Data_31864;
_UIBANNER_2 = Data_31864;
_UIBANNER_3 = Data_31864;
